WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.210
Dr. Lola Bergman was an accomplished senior
researcher for the SCP Foundation.

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:08.950
Her years of hard work studying anomalies
and dedication to the Foundation had earned

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:13.710
her a Level 3 security clearance, allowing
her to access a bevy of secret information

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.209
across the Foundation’s vast network.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:20.800
As a researcher and scientist, Dr. Bergman
was the naturally inquisitive type.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.680
She wanted to know everything there was to
know about the strange and esoteric universe

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:25.680
around her.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.700
That’s why, when she heard about the file
for SCP – 2000, one of the organization’s

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.420
best kept secrets, she was hooked.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:37.900
The only problem was that just to view the
file, you had to be on the O5 council or have

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.380
a special subset of Level 4 clearance known
as 4/2000.

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:45.040
Unfortunately, Dr. Bergman was only a Level
3.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.900
But you know what they say: The forbidden
fruit always tastes the sweetest.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.460
Dr. Bergman had given the Foundation years
of service.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:56.850
What could the harm be in breaking the rules
and getting a peek behind the curtain just

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:57.850
this once?

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:03.480
Through somewhat deceptive means, Dr. Bergman
managed to get her hands on some 4/2000 clearance

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.370
credentials, and decided to access the file
on an encrypted personal device.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:08.810
They’d never even know.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:13.070
But as she entered the credentials, she found
herself suddenly looking at an image.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:19.790
On its face, nothing, an image of a dirt road
in a field, with tiny white text reading “…Remember

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:20.790
us…”

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:26.070
But this was a powerful cognitohazardous image
capable of causing immediate incapacitation.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:30.000
The next thing she knew, she was laying on
the ground, unconscious.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.310
When she woke up, she was no longer a doctor
at the SCP Foundation.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:39.650
She was on a street corner, unable to remember
her own name, let alone her history.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:44.140
All she could do was murmur something about
a secret under Yellowstone National Park…

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.110
Dr. Bergman’s years of service had meant
nothing in the end.

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:53.790
SCP – 2000 is an anomalous asset so vital
to not only the Foundation, but the entire

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:58.940
world, that so much as attempting to access
it without the proper clearance leads to immediate

00:01:58.940 --> 00:01:59.940
termination.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:03.159
But what’s really hidden under Yellowstone
National Park?

00:02:03.159 --> 00:02:07.590
And why are the Foundation higher-ups so intent
on keeping it a secret, even from most of

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:08.640
their own ranks?

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.580
Of course, Yellowstone National Park itself
is no stranger to anomalous activity.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:17.660
The entire national park is technically an
SCP known as SCP – 1422.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:25.420
While it has since been neutralized, SCP-1422
caused all members of the SCP Foundation to

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:30.390
have no awareness of Yellowstone National
Park prior to 2007.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:36.060
But the true nature of SCP – 2000, the secret
hidden under Yellowstone National Park, is

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:42.150
not only stranger than SCP – 1422, but also
– depending on your perspective – either

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.810
profoundly relieving or utterly terrifying.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:51.020
But before you can truly understand the purpose
and gravity of SCP – 2000, or the Deus Ex

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:54.750
Machina, we need to talk about the end of
the world.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:03:00.040
In Foundation parlance, K-class scenarios
are events which have drastic implications

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:03.670
on the state of society, normality, or even
life.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.981
The most dangerous and feared of all of these
are XK – Class Scenarios, which are, for

00:03:07.981 --> 00:03:11.490
all intents and purposes, the apocalypse.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:16.300
There are a worrying number of anomalies capable
of bringing about an XK Class scenario if

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.760
the Foundation ever dropped the ball on their
protection or containment.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.390
While the Foundation believes that prevention
is always preferable to the cure, what happens

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.379
when prevention doesn’t work?

00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:31.140
What happens when our worst fears are realized,
and the end truly comes for us all?

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:35.650
The answer is, SCP – 2000 happens.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:38.100
Simply put, this anomaly is a reset button.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.840
A chance to start over when all life has been
compromised by anomalous activity.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.620
2000 is a giant and mysterious facility beneath
Yellowstone National Park.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:52.069
The entrance is disguised as a disused park
ranger station, feeding into a facility that

00:03:52.069 --> 00:03:54.069
burrows miles down into the earth.

00:03:54.069 --> 00:03:58.489
It’s a place that contains incredible anomalous
technologies capable of bringing the world

00:03:58.489 --> 00:04:02.569
back to its former glory, even after the worst
happens.

00:04:02.569 --> 00:04:07.099
Only a select few personnel are allowed to
work at the facility, keeping its machinery

00:04:07.099 --> 00:04:11.660
maintained in case the day comes when we ever
need to use it again.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:15.760
These personnel are not allowed to come and
go as they please and their memories of 2000

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.489
are wiped if they’re reassigned to different
Foundation duties elsewhere.

00:04:19.489 --> 00:04:21.150
Why all the secrecy?

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:27.050
SCP – 2000 is the Foundation’s – and
humanity’s – lifeboat, and as such, the

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:32.159
base is well-guarded both from potential human
threats, as well as anomalous activity and

00:04:32.159 --> 00:04:33.770
reality warpers.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:37.650
The surface-level entrance to the base is
surrounded by a number of strategically placed

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.430
Scranton Reality Anchors, or SRAs.

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:45.719
These devices, largely constructed of corrosion-resistant
beryllium bronze alloy, keep certain types

00:04:45.719 --> 00:04:50.880
of reality-bending anomalies from manifesting
their powers within the vicinity of the base.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.330
However, these are just the tip of the iceberg
when it comes to the state-of-the-art Foundation

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:59.809
technology employed within and around SCP
– 2000.

00:04:59.809 --> 00:05:03.819
You probably have a lot of questions, and
that’s understandable: What kind of technology

00:05:03.819 --> 00:05:06.009
can possibly reverse an apocalypse?

00:05:06.009 --> 00:05:09.139
And what did we mean when we said we may need
to use it “again”?

00:05:09.139 --> 00:05:13.550
Well listen up, because this is going to be
a lot to take in.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:20.460
SCP – 2000 is powered by a Liquid Fluoride
Thorium Reactor, stabilized by five Xyank/Anastasakos

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:27.419
Constant Temporal Sinks, and host to the most
miraculous technology of all: 500,000 Bright/Zartion

00:05:27.419 --> 00:05:29.119
Hominid Replicators.

00:05:29.119 --> 00:05:33.639
Utilizing geothermal energy from the volcanic
activity in the area, the base could potentially

00:05:33.639 --> 00:05:38.009
remain operational forever if Foundation staff
kept up with maintenance.

00:05:38.009 --> 00:05:42.479
The grand majority of the space in this huge,
subterranean facility is taken up by building

00:05:42.479 --> 00:05:47.599
materials, construction equipment, factory
machinery, agricultural equipment, and computer

00:05:47.599 --> 00:05:48.969
database storage.

00:05:48.969 --> 00:05:49.969
Why?

00:05:49.969 --> 00:05:54.129
Well, let’s explain the processes of the
apocalypse and how it relates to the activities

00:05:54.129 --> 00:05:56.050
of SCP – 2000.

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:59.780
In any K-Class or end of the world scenario
that doesn’t destroy SCP – 2000 in the

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:07.110
process, any surviving Foundation member can
enact Procedure CYA-009, while any Foundation

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:10.300
facilities globally can monitor the unfolding
situation.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:15.550
While SCP – 2000 is waiting for the “all
clear” to do its thing, remaining Foundation

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:19.089
sites set into motion what is known as the
“Ganymede Protocol.”

00:06:19.089 --> 00:06:24.219
This involves collecting and preserving materials
which could be useful during the recreation

00:06:24.219 --> 00:06:25.219
process.

00:06:25.219 --> 00:06:29.949
However, it’s extremely possible that in
a sudden XK class end of the world scenario

00:06:29.949 --> 00:06:35.639
there may be no surviving Foundation personnel,
or at least not enough to maintain any kind

00:06:35.639 --> 00:06:36.820
of organization.

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:41.300
Thankfully, there are fail safe measures for
such a scenario.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:45.559
During the periods of extended inaction caused
by an apocalypse that destroys most human

00:06:45.559 --> 00:06:51.139
life, SCP – 2000 will gradually, autonomously
relax its security measures.

00:06:51.139 --> 00:06:54.990
Any personnel, regardless of clearance, will
be able to activate the next stage of the

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.900
process when this occurs.

00:06:56.900 --> 00:07:01.429
If such a thing doesn’t happen for an extended
period, the security measures lapse further,

00:07:01.429 --> 00:07:04.719
to the point that even the presence of an
animal could activate it.

00:07:04.719 --> 00:07:10.000
This would set into motion Procedure Lazarus-01,
humanity’s last hope for survival.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:15.699
That’s where the 500,000 Bright/Zartion
Hominid Replicators, or BZHRs, come in.

00:07:15.699 --> 00:07:20.539
These incredible pieces of anomalous technology
are capable of perfectly cloning the entire

00:07:20.539 --> 00:07:26.300
human race from any given period in the last
twenty years, even down to their ages and

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:30.770
memories, thanks to Class-G hallucinogens
and developmental hypnotherapy.

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:36.319
With a warm-up period of five days, these
machines are capable of creating 100,000 viable,

00:07:36.319 --> 00:07:38.490
non-anomalous humans per day.

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:42.789
The facility keeps records of all known human
alleles, and is capable of recreating any

00:07:42.789 --> 00:07:48.449
lost human genomes or generating as many new
and unique genomes as necessary to repopulate

00:07:48.449 --> 00:07:50.349
human civilization.

00:07:50.349 --> 00:07:54.309
Which humans the machines generate depends
on the period of time the machine is ordered

00:07:54.309 --> 00:07:55.309
to replicate.

00:07:55.309 --> 00:07:59.319
It will first reproduce prominent cultural
and political figures, as well as the acting

00:07:59.319 --> 00:08:04.090
top brass of the SCP Foundation to coordinate
the recreation effort, of course.

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:07.569
The machine will then recreate…Well, everyone
else.

00:08:07.569 --> 00:08:12.719
You’re probably wondering: If the world
outside SCP – 2000 has been destroyed then

00:08:12.719 --> 00:08:16.159
what good is creating all these new humans
if they have nothing to go back to?

00:08:16.159 --> 00:08:21.009
Thankfully, SCP – 2000 has a contingency
for rebuilding both the infrastructure of

00:08:21.009 --> 00:08:25.169
civilization as well as society itself.

00:08:25.169 --> 00:08:30.270
The first generation of clones will have one
mission: Rebuild, and they have quite literally

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:33.210
all the resources in the world to do it.

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:38.700
In addition to having all the physical equipment
for buildings and agriculture, SCP – 2000

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.640
stores an immense amount of data.

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:44.800
Which is appropriate, considering the whole
facility is basically a huge backup drive

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:46.760
for life itself.

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:51.700
They have the entire internet backed up, as
well as the sum of all human memory, and a

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:57.080
wide cultural base with copies of thousands
of famous works of art, music, and literature.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:01.371
They have all the scattered jigsaw pieces
of the time that came before, and it’s their

00:09:01.371 --> 00:09:03.770
job to put the whole thing back together.

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:08.900
It’s expected that many of the first generation
of clones will die in this process, but that’s

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:09.900
fine.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:14.490
With the BZHRs working at full capacity, anyone
can be replaced.

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:19.450
As the population increases and spreads across
the globe, rebuilding and recolonizing old

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:23.440
settlements, population growth will increase
exponentially.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:28.280
Once society returns to some kind of normal,
decades after the activation of Procedure

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:33.010
Lazarus 01, it becomes the Foundation’s
task to restore the psychological and historical

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:34.010
status quo.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:39.190
Administrators will falsify dendrochronological,
astronomical, and radiometric dating records

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:41.840
to make it seem as though history never paused.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:47.570
They’ll then employ amnestic agent ENUI-5
en masse to erase and reconstruct the memories

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:52.820
of the entire human population, up until the
chosen time prior to the end of the world.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:58.820
As far as any civilian knows, nothing ever
happened, history never stopped, and life

00:09:58.820 --> 00:09:59.820
goes on.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:04.550
It may seem comforting at first to know that
the ultimate contingency is out there.

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:08.400
Even if there’s a containment breach of
astronomical proportions, and the world as

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.740
we know it ends, there’s still a way out.

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:17.071
It means that there’s hope, even in the
bleakest situations, but of course, there

00:10:17.071 --> 00:10:19.060
are always caveats.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:24.590
After all, if a deal seems too good to be
true, it’s safe to assume it probably is.

00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:29.330
Whether any of this works is dependent on
two factors: The first is that no anomalies

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:34.680
or human insurgents infiltrate the SCP – 2000
facility and destroy the equipment within.

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:38.980
It’s for this reason that discussion of
the facility is so restrictive – if ever

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:44.060
a powerful reality-warper discovered and gained
access to the facility, we would lose our

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:45.680
lifeboat.

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:49.070
The second factor is the overall integrity
of the machines.

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:53.430
After all, without proper maintenance, it’s
not hard for the technology to malfunction,

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:56.360
often to truly horrifying results.

00:10:56.360 --> 00:11:01.890
The failure of some SRA and XACTS components
is believed to have caused erroneous BZHR

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:06.810
activity, resulting in the production of ten
million humanoid entities with internal biology

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:09.640
wildly inconsistent with humanity.

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:12.850
These beings remained unconscious for five
weeks before dying.

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:18.080
In the event of an XK class end of the world
scenario, well-maintained machines can reproduce

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:19.390
the human race.

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:24.340
But all it’d take is one little malfunction
to release ten million mutants who are effectively

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:26.550
dead on arrival.

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:32.310
Even with SCP – 2000 in our back pocket,
the fate of the world still lives on a knife’s

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:33.310
edge.

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:37.850
But in a way, that’s not even the most frightening
part of the existence of SCP – 2000.

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:42.420
Seeing as the machine can perfectly reproduce
reality without leaving a trace behind, it

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:46.500
raises the question: How many times has this
happened?

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:51.730
You may be surprised, but the Foundation can
confirm this occurring at least twice in recorded

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:56.330
history, but can you ever really be sure of
how many times your clone has crawled out

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:58.340
of the BZHR?

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:02.870
We received a disturbing clue to the reality
of the situation when human remains were discovered

00:12:02.870 --> 00:12:05.530
on site at SCP – 2000.

00:12:05.530 --> 00:12:11.560
All tests indicate the remains are between
450 and 700 years old, and have been matched

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:16.240
to the very-much-alive Dr. Alto Clef, who
has no knowledge of how these remains came

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:17.520
to be there.

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:21.900
The only other thing found with the remains
was a note, hermetically sealed in a plastic

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:23.430
document sleeve.

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:26.960
The note read:
“Why did we have to build this thing?

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:28.620
When did we do it?

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:31.470
How long have we been doing it?

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:33.720
Do we even know?!”

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:34.800
And the answer?

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:36.320
No, we don’t.

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:41.570
And for everyone’s piece of mind, it’s
probably best to just forget about it.

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:42.570
If you can…

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:49.370
Now go check out “SCP – 096 The Shy Guy”
and “SCP – 087 The Stairwell” for more

00:12:49.370 --> 00:12:53.350
strange and surprising stories from the SCP
Foundation.

